1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,670
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:06,936
This is chapter twenty-one: sequenced collections.

3
00:00:07,386 --> 00:00:13,961
For twenty-five years, Java had ordered collections but no common way to talk about their ends.

4
00:00:13,961 --> 00:00:17,560
Java 21 fixed that by retrofitting three interfaces.

5
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:25,242
The feature is small, the retrofit is interesting, and there's one correction that circulating notes get wrong.

6
00:00:26,142 --> 00:00:32,295
Before Java 21, the last element of four ordered collections needed four spellings.

7
00:00:32,295 --> 00:00:34,815
A List used get of size minus one.

8
00:00:34,815 --> 00:00:36,298
A Deque had getLast.

9
00:00:36,298 --> 00:00:41,931
A LinkedHashSet had no accessor at all, so you looped through every element.

10
00:00:41,931 --> 00:00:43,785
And a SortedSet had last.

11
00:00:44,235 --> 00:00:45,963
Going backwards was worse.

12
00:00:45,963 --> 00:00:49,154
You copied the whole list and reversed the copy.

13
00:00:49,154 --> 00:00:53,075
And for a LinkedHashMap, there was no supported way at all.

14
00:00:53,075 --> 00:00:59,723
Each type had some idea of first and last, but no shared interface, so no generic code could use it.

15
00:01:00,623 --> 00:01:02,997
Java 21 added three interfaces.

16
00:01:02,997 --> 00:01:12,724
SequencedCollection gives every ordered collection getFirst, getLast, addFirst, addLast, removeFirst, removeLast, and reversed.

17
00:01:12,724 --> 00:01:14,639
List and Deque extend it.

18
00:01:15,089 --> 00:01:19,702
SequencedSet adds nothing new, except that reversed returns a Set.

19
00:01:19,702 --> 00:01:22,427
LinkedHashSet and TreeSet implement it.

20
00:01:22,877 --> 00:01:29,664
And SequencedMap gives firstEntry, lastEntry, putFirst, putLast, poll methods, and reversed.

21
00:01:29,664 --> 00:01:32,541
LinkedHashMap and TreeMap implement it.

22
00:01:32,991 --> 00:01:36,163
The defining property is an encounter order.

23
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,037
And here's the correction.

24
00:01:38,037 --> 00:01:40,775
HashMap and HashSet are not sequenced.

25
00:01:40,775 --> 00:01:45,748
Their iteration order is unspecified, so there's no meaningful first.

26
00:01:45,748 --> 00:01:51,586
Notes and blog posts claiming Java 21 gave HashMap a firstEntry method are wrong.

27
00:01:51,586 --> 00:01:56,415
PriorityQueue is excluded too, because its iteration is heap order.

28
00:01:57,315 --> 00:01:59,040
Now it's one vocabulary.

29
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:03,641
getLast works on a list, a deque, a LinkedHashSet and a TreeSet.

30
00:02:03,641 --> 00:02:11,331
The LinkedHashSet case is the big win: it's O of one now, because it keeps a doubly-linked list internally.

31
00:02:11,781 --> 00:02:14,247
And the real payoff is generic code.

32
00:02:14,247 --> 00:02:23,494
A method can accept any SequencedCollection and take its last element, whether you pass an ArrayList, an ArrayDeque or a LinkedHashSet.

33
00:02:23,494 --> 00:02:29,659
Before Java 21, that method couldn't be written, because no type meant ordered collection.

34
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:37,452
The single most important thing about this feature: reversed returns a live, writable view, not a copy.

35
00:02:37,452 --> 00:02:40,933
It's O of one to create, however big the collection.

36
00:02:40,933 --> 00:02:47,559
But writing through the view changes the original, and changes to the original show up in the view.

37
00:02:47,559 --> 00:02:48,964
Exactly like subList.

38
00:02:48,964 --> 00:02:52,980
If you need an independent copy, wrap it in a new ArrayList.

39
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:55,834
That makes reverse iteration free.

40
00:02:55,834 --> 00:02:57,459
No copy, no allocation.

41
00:02:57,459 --> 00:03:04,246
And it finally works on a LinkedHashMap, which had no supported way to iterate backwards before.

42
00:03:05,146 --> 00:03:06,825
Here's a practical use.

43
00:03:06,825 --> 00:03:13,977
putFirst on a LinkedHashMap inserts a new key at the front, or moves an existing key to the front.

44
00:03:13,977 --> 00:03:22,005
Combine it with pollLastEntry to evict the oldest, and a most-recently-used structure is a few readable lines.

45
00:03:22,005 --> 00:03:28,719
Before, it meant LinkedHashMap's access-order constructor plus overriding removeEldestEntry.

46
00:03:29,619 --> 00:03:31,230
Some operations throw.

47
00:03:31,230 --> 00:03:39,134
A TreeSet happily gives you getFirst and a reversed view, but addFirst throws UnsupportedOperationException.

48
00:03:39,134 --> 00:03:42,867
And of course immutable lists reject every mutator.

49
00:03:43,317 --> 00:03:44,279
That's correct.

50
00:03:44,279 --> 00:03:50,687
A sorted collection decides position with its comparator, so insert at the front is a contradiction.

51
00:03:50,687 --> 00:03:59,595
Putting it in sorted position instead would make addFirst mean different things on different collections, which defeats a shared interface.

52
00:04:00,045 --> 00:04:01,615
And note an asymmetry.

53
00:04:01,615 --> 00:04:10,967
getFirst on an empty collection throws, while firstEntry on an empty map returns null, following the older NavigableMap convention.

54
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:18,194
How do you add methods to List in 2023 without breaking every implementation out there?

55
00:04:18,194 --> 00:04:19,358
Default methods.

56
00:04:19,358 --> 00:04:24,085
getFirst defaults to get zero, and getLast to get size minus one.

57
00:04:24,085 --> 00:04:26,485
Third-party lists keep compiling.

58
00:04:26,485 --> 00:04:29,830
JDK classes override where they can do better.

59
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:34,630
It's the same mechanism that made Java 8's stream method possible.

60
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,763
Deque was already most of the way there.

61
00:04:37,763 --> 00:04:42,995
It's had addFirst and getLast since Java 6, with exactly the right signatures.

62
00:04:43,445 --> 00:04:45,605
And reversed joins a family.

63
00:04:45,605 --> 00:04:50,312
subList, keySet, values, unmodifiableList, Arrays dot asList.

64
00:04:50,312 --> 00:04:56,793
Almost everything in java dot util that returns a derived collection returns a view.

65
00:04:56,793 --> 00:05:01,962
The copies are the ones with copy, or a constructor, in their name.

66
00:05:02,862 --> 00:05:04,109
Production rules.

67
00:05:04,109 --> 00:05:07,995
Use getFirst and getLast instead of index arithmetic.

68
00:05:07,995 --> 00:05:10,561
Use reversed for reverse iteration.

69
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:13,715
Accept SequencedCollection in generic code.

70
00:05:13,715 --> 00:05:18,041
Use putFirst and pollLastEntry for most-recently-used maps.

71
00:05:18,041 --> 00:05:22,147
And wrap reversed in a constructor when you need a copy.

72
00:05:22,597 --> 00:05:26,212
Never expect these methods on HashMap or HashSet.

73
00:05:26,212 --> 00:05:32,852
Never treat reversed as a copy, or store the view in a field unless you want the aliasing.

74
00:05:32,852 --> 00:05:35,287
Don't call addFirst on a TreeSet.

75
00:05:35,287 --> 00:05:38,533
And check for empty before calling getFirst.

76
00:05:39,433 --> 00:05:40,123
To recap.

77
00:05:40,123 --> 00:05:45,183
Java 21 gave every ordered collection one vocabulary for its ends.

78
00:05:45,183 --> 00:05:50,856
HashMap and HashSet aren't included, because they have no encounter order.

79
00:05:50,856 --> 00:05:54,382
reversed is an O of one live view, not a copy.

80
00:05:54,382 --> 00:05:58,292
And sorted collections correctly throw on addFirst.

81
00:05:58,742 --> 00:06:03,065
Next is chapter twenty-two: immutability and defensive copying.

82
00:06:03,065 --> 00:06:04,438
Thanks for watching.
